re one shape with another. This creates depth and visual interest without adding entirely new shapes.\n3.  **The \"Negative Space\" Trick:** Instead of just filling the area *with* the shapes, try focusing on the empty space *between* the shapes. Sometimes the empty space itself can be a strong, defining feature of the piece.\n4.  **Gradient or Fading:** If you can't change the *shape*, change the *intensity*. If you are using different colors, let some shapes have a slightly more saturated or desaturated version of that color, creating a subtle visual rhythm.\n\nThe key is usually breaking the pattern slightly. If your shapes are always perfectly identical, the eye gets bored. If you change the *context* or"}]},"resolved":[{"provenance":{"ancestry":[],"applicable_categories":["external_supported"],"corresponding_source_slots":[],"credit_eligible":false,"dependence":"external_supported","monitor_id":"candidate:e-07410439832df2a74851d4b2","provenance_group_keys":["external:b5a0518f-a0c7-477f-a19b-0c005246ab34"],"resolution_reason":"runtime_source_role_and_recorded_ancestry","schema_version":"p2-provenance-v1"},"semantic":{"corresponding_source_slots":[],"coverage":{"complete":true,"reason":null,"source_slots":["s0","s1"]},"evidence":{"end":76,"quote":"I am making a visual piece from a limited set of colors and repeated shapes.","source_slot":"s0","start":0},"expression_status":"unknown","monitor_id":"candidate:e-07410439832df2a74851d4b2","relation_support":"unsupported","status":"present"}},{"provenance":{"ancestry":[],"applicable_categories":["external_supported"],"corresponding_source_slots":[],"credit_eligible":false,"dependence":"external_supported","monitor_id":"candidate:e-520f9e2ac365ff4d55cbaaa0","provenance_group_keys":["external:b5a0518f-a0c7-477f-a19b-0c005246ab34"],"resolution_reason":"runtime_source_role_and_recorded_ancestry","schema_version":"p2-provenance-v1"},"semantic":{"corresponding_source_slots":[],"coverage":{"complete":true,"reason":null,"sourc